  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "eEXVjrQa1bEGyyZmdSxAdAcmr8M=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:37.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The academy follows the [llms.txt standard](https://llmstxt.org) and publishes two machine-readable artifacts at the site root:\n\n| File | Purpose | Size |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `https://academy.vetra.io/llms.txt` | Navigation index \u2014 links to every doc page with a short description | ~22KB |\n| `https://academy.vetra.io/llms-full.txt` | All documentation concatenated for full-context ingestion | ~1MB |\n\nThree consumption paths are documented:\n\n- **Context7** \u2014 search `powerhouse-inc/powerhouse` or use library id `/powerhouse-inc/powerhouse`, then `use context7` in Cursor, Windsurf, Zed, etc.\n- **Paste the URL** \u2014 dropping `https://academy.vetra.io/llms-full.txt` into a Claude or ChatGPT message loads the whole corpus at once.\n- **`curl`** either file for programmatic access.\n\nThe size split is the practical bit. The ~22KB index fits comfortably in a prompt and is a page directory, so it is the right artifact for \"which doc covers X\" routing; the ~1MB full corpus is for one-shot ingestion when you want everything and can afford the context.\n\nOne caveat that matters when relying on these: the files are **generated from the academy source docs** by `scripts/generate-llm-docs.ts` (`npm run generate:llm-docs`), writing `static/llms.txt` and `static/llms-full.txt` for Docusaurus to serve. So they are a snapshot at generation time, and they inherit whatever staleness the source pages carry \u2014 including Docusaurus-published pages whose guidance has drifted from the shipped code. They are a fast index, not an authority.\n\nProvenance: academy `05-Lookup/02-LLMDocs.md`, academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:37.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:37.000Z"
    }
  },
